B2, then B4, Prerequisites:

8
00:00:16.409 --> 00:00:35.673
Ch 05 (isolation, row locks), Ch 15 (event sourcing, projections), Ch 46 (holds and TTLs), Ch 47 (rider-minutes, ETAs), What you will build: slot-service, which answers which delivery slots can I have, and can I book this one? for scheduled grocery orders.

9
00:00:35.953 --> 00:00:53.020
That means capacity that means something, bookings that can't overbook under a Diwali-eve rush, an event-sourced slot aggregate with a history you can audit and rebuild (the design Ch 15 deferred here), and slot screens and prices that move demand to where the riders are.

10
00:00:53.300 --> 00:01:02.068
It is also the classic design a booking system (tickets, appointments, hotel rooms) interview question, with capacity that isn't a simple count.

11
00:01:03.418 --> 00:01:04.485
The four labs:

12
00:01:04.765 --> 00:01:07.005
L1, what full means (simulation).

13
00:01:07.285 --> 00:01:08.906
One zone over 30 days:

14
00:01:09.186 --> 00:01:18.999
A cap of 40 orders per slot accepted 9,321 orders and delivered 2,444 late (26%).

15
00:01:19.279 --> 00:01:28.474
Capacity in rider-minutes from the roster, with each booking deducting its estimated cost (including evening traffic), delivered 21 late.

16
00:01:29.824 --> 00:01:36.373
The number delivered on time stayed about the same (~6,900 to 7,050).

17
00:01:36.653 --> 00:01:41.496
The rule decides whether customers hear full at booking time or sorry, late at the door.

18
00:01:41.776 --> 00:01:45.317
L2, bookings under contention (real PostgreSQL).

19
00:01:45.597 --> 00:01:51.827
32 workers made 12,000 attempts on 60 slots, 70% of them on 8 hot slots:

20
00:01:52.107 --> 00:02:01.877
Read-check-write in Java overbooked all 60 slots, with 2,965 bookings against ~2,000 slots' worth of capacity.

21
00:02:02.157 --> 00:02:06.701
SERIALIZABLE isolation fixed it with 1,043 retries.

22
00:02:08.051 --> 00:02:09.481
A conditional UPDATE...

23
00:02:09.761 --> 00:02:15.819
WHERE used + m = cap fixed it with 0 retries and the best throughput and p99.

24
00:02:16.099 --> 00:02:23.331
An event-sourced append guarded by PK(slot, version) was also exact, with 1,020 retries.

25
00:02:23.611 --> 00:02:31.569
L3, the slot as an event-sourced aggregate (real PostgreSQL, 819,306 events).

26
00:02:31.849 --> 00:02:39.806
A projector bug made 2,088 of 3,000 slots wrong, and 442 were shown as full while they had room.

27
00:02:40.086 --> 00:02:44.822
Rebuilding the read model from the events took ~1 s and fixed all of them.

28
00:02:46.172 --> 00:02:55.964
Snapshots cut loading a hot slot from 2,800 rows (1.4 ms p50) to 52 rows (0.12 ms).

29
00:02:56.244 --> 00:03:01.343
What did this slot look like earlier and whose hold filled it are 3 to 12 ms queries.

30
00:03:01.623 --> 00:03:04.033
L4, moving demand (simulation).

31
00:03:04.313 --> 00:03:05.615
With the same riders:

32
00:03:05.895 --> 00:03:16.583
Offering the nearest free slots, plus peak fees and shoulder discounts, cut lost customers from 1,631 to 1,357.

33
00:03:16.863 --> 00:03:22.068
Moving the same rider-hours into the peak cut them to 1,242.

34
00:03:23.418 --> 00:03:26.405
The acceptance rates are stated assumptions (VERIFY).

