Part 1.

38
00:03:32.051 --> 00:03:33.971
Introduction and Why It Matters.

39
00:03:35.221 --> 00:03:38.698
A story: the 7 pm slot that was always available.

40
00:03:38.978 --> 00:03:44.439
The first slot system had a table slot(zone, start, maxorders=40, booked).

41
00:03:44.719 --> 00:03:55.109
The app showed slots with booked maxorders; checkout did SELECT booked...; if (booked 40) INSERT booking; UPDATE slot SET booked = bookedread + 1.

42
00:03:55.389 --> 00:03:56.839
It went wrong in four ways:

43
00:03:57.119 --> 00:03:58.421
Full didn't mean full.

44
00:03:58.701 --> 00:04:02.285
Forty orders at 11 am took 25 minutes each.

45
00:04:02.565 --> 00:04:08.282
Forty orders at 7 pm, in the jam, to towers with security desks, took 45.

46
00:04:08.562 --> 00:04:16.071
The 7 pm slot was available long after the riders were fully booked, and a quarter of those orders arrived after their window.

47
00:04:17.421 --> 00:04:19.533
Diwali eve overbooked every slot.

48
00:04:19.813 --> 00:04:35.024
Slots for the festival opened at 6 am; thousands of customers booked in the same second, two checkouts read the same booked and both wrote booked + 1, and some slots ended up with 60 orders on a 40-order counter that said 41.

49
00:04:35.304 --> 00:04:36.989
Nobody could say what happened.

50
00:04:37.269 --> 00:04:41.024
Why was I told the 6 pm slot was full at 9:14?

51
00:04:41.304 --> 00:04:48.451
The counter said 40; the bookings table said 38; there was no history of holds, cancellations and reschedules.

52
00:04:48.731 --> 00:04:50.971
Support refunded and apologised.

53
00:04:52.321 --> 00:04:54.070
Everyone wanted 7 pm.

54
00:04:54.350 --> 00:04:58.425
Afternoon riders sat idle while evening customers were turned away.

55
00:04:58.705 --> 00:05:02.651
The app greyed out full slots and customers left instead of scrolling.

56
00:05:02.931 --> 00:05:05.641
Slots are a promise made hours or days ahead.

57
00:05:05.921 --> 00:05:13.729
This chapter designs what a slot's capacity means, how to take it safely, how to keep its history, and how to fill the day evenly.

58
00:05:15.079 --> 00:05:15.761
Quote:

59
00:05:16.041 --> 00:05:17.705
The thesis of this chapter.

60
00:05:17.985 --> 00:05:30.145
A delivery slot is capacity in the unit that actually runs out (rider-minutes from the roster, picker-minutes in the store), consumed by bookings that each deduct their own estimated cost (L1).

61
00:05:30.425 --> 00:05:39.108
Taking capacity must be one atomic step (a conditional update or a version-guarded append), because check-then-act overbooks (L2).

62
00:05:39.388 --> 00:05:46.897
The slot's history is the source of truth: events rebuild the read model after bugs and answer audit questions (L3).

63
00:05:47.177 --> 00:05:57.759
And capacity is only half the problem: showing the right alternatives and pricing peaks moves demand to where the riders are, and the roster should follow demand too (L4).

64
