333
00:19:53.747 --> 00:20:00.979
Price the peak: fees and discounts move flexible customers before the peak fills, leaving room for those who can't move.

334
00:20:01.259 --> 00:20:05.121
Roster to demand: the same rider-hours placed where demand is.

335
00:20:05.401 --> 00:20:08.558
Slot booking data is the best input to rostering.

336
00:20:08.838 --> 00:20:17.307
Green or batched slots: discounts for slots where the rider is already going to the same building (batching, Ch 47 L3).

337
00:20:18.657 --> 00:20:27.681
The acceptance and price-response rates in L4 are assumptions (VERIFY); measure them with experiments (switchback per zone, since slots interact).

338
00:20:29.031 --> 00:20:30.738
Listing availability fast.

339
00:20:31.018 --> 00:20:39.359
The listing is read-heavy and tolerates seconds of staleness: serve free minutes per slot for the zone from Redis, updated by the projector.

340
00:20:39.639 --> 00:20:44.482
Showing available doesn't guarantee booking: the booking command is the exact check.

341
00:20:44.762 --> 00:20:49.157
Show almost full when free minutes are below a threshold, to set expectations.

342
00:20:49.437 --> 00:20:56.242
Filter per order: a slot with 20 free minutes can take a 15-minute order but not a 35-minute one.

343
00:20:56.522 --> 00:20:59.850
Compute availability with this order's estimated cost.

344
00:21:01.200 --> 00:21:04.379
Rescheduling, cancellations and capacity changes.

345
00:21:04.659 --> 00:21:11.571
Reschedule = book the new slot first, then release the old (never the reverse, or the customer can lose both).

346
00:21:11.851 --> 00:21:17.845
Model it as a saga across two aggregates, or one transaction if both live in one database.

347
00:21:18.125 --> 00:21:30.499
Capacity drops (rider sick, rain) can leave slots over-committed: flag them, protect already-booked orders, stop new holds, and ask dispatch to borrow riders or notify customers early.

348
00:21:30.779 --> 00:21:36.773
Cut-offs: stop booking a slot when picking must start; close slots in zones affected by events.

349
00:21:38.123 --> 00:21:39.254
Interview framing.

350
00:21:39.534 --> 00:21:46.403
This is the classic design a booking system question (tickets, hotel rooms, doctor appointments, restaurant tables).

351
00:21:46.683 --> 00:22:02.299
Cover: the unit of capacity;. atomic capacity checks under contention;. holds with TTL;. history and auditing (event sourcing as an option, with its costs);. fast availability reads with exact booking;. rescheduling as a saga;.

352
00:22:03.649 --> 00:22:04.780
demand shaping.

353
00:22:05.060 --> 00:22:14.041
Staff-level signals are saying count is the wrong unit, separating browse staleness from booking exactness, and connecting booking data to rostering.

