560
00:41:09.169 --> 00:41:10.107
Part 6.

561
00:41:10.387 --> 00:41:11.667
Production Concerns.

562
00:41:12.917 --> 00:41:14.069
Failure modes.

563
00:41:14.349 --> 00:41:15.693
Here is a quick table:

564
00:41:15.973 --> 00:41:18.256
Failure, and Symptom, and Mitigation.

565
00:41:18.536 --> 00:41:19.944
Count-based capacity.

566
00:41:20.224 --> 00:41:21.035
Symptom:

567
00:41:21.315 --> 00:41:24.408
Late evening deliveries, idle afternoons (L1).

568
00:41:24.688 --> 00:41:25.691
Mitigation:

569
00:41:25.971 --> 00:41:28.232
Rider or picker-minutes, per-order cost.

570
00:41:28.512 --> 00:41:29.600
Check-then-act.

571
00:41:29.880 --> 00:41:30.691
Symptom:

572
00:41:30.971 --> 00:41:33.701
Overbooked slots at festival peaks (L2).

573
00:41:33.981 --> 00:41:34.984
Mitigation:

574
00:41:35.264 --> 00:41:37.888
Conditional update or version-guarded append.

575
00:41:38.168 --> 00:41:39.768
SERIALIZABLE everywhere.

576
00:41:40.048 --> 00:41:40.859
Symptom:

577
00:41:41.139 --> 00:41:43.507
Abort storms on hot slots (L2).

578
00:41:43.787 --> 00:41:44.789
Mitigation:

579
00:41:45.069 --> 00:41:46.691
Targeted atomic statements.

580
00:41:46.971 --> 00:41:48.037
Projector bug.

581
00:41:48.317 --> 00:41:49.128
Symptom:

582
00:41:49.408 --> 00:41:51.584
Slots shown full with room (L3).

583
00:41:51.864 --> 00:41:52.867
Mitigation:

584
00:41:53.147 --> 00:41:56.901
Rebuild from events; daily audit of read model against events.

585
00:41:57.181 --> 00:41:58.696
Hot slot long history.

586
00:41:58.976 --> 00:41:59.787
Symptom:

587
00:42:00.067 --> 00:42:02.093
Slow aggregate loads (L3).

588
00:42:02.373 --> 00:42:03.376
Mitigation:

589
00:42:03.656 --> 00:42:04.637
Snapshots.

590
00:42:05.987 --> 00:42:07.161
Abandoned holds.

591
00:42:07.441 --> 00:42:08.251
Symptom:

592
00:42:08.531 --> 00:42:09.854
Phantom full slots.

593
00:42:10.134 --> 00:42:11.137
Mitigation:

594
00:42:11.417 --> 00:42:14.169
Short TTLs, extension on payment start.

595
00:42:14.449 --> 00:42:16.283
Capacity drop after booking.

596
00:42:16.563 --> 00:42:17.374
Symptom:

597
00:42:17.654 --> 00:42:18.934
Over-committed slots.

598
00:42:19.214 --> 00:42:20.217
Mitigation:

599
00:42:20.497 --> 00:42:24.273
Flag, protect booked orders, borrow riders, notify early.

600
00:42:24.553 --> 00:42:25.683
Stale listings.

601
00:42:25.963 --> 00:42:26.774
Symptom:

602
00:42:27.054 --> 00:42:28.675
Available then full at hold.

603
00:42:28.955 --> 00:42:29.958
Mitigation:

604
00:42:30.238 --> 00:42:33.481
Show almost full; recommend alternatives on failure.

605
00:42:34.831 --> 00:42:35.919
Observability.

606
00:42:36.199 --> 00:42:42.151
Overbooking audit: sum of booked minutes versus capacity per slot (should be 0 violations).

607
00:42:42.431 --> 00:42:47.380
Hold outcomes: confirm rate, expiry rate, late payments after expiry.

608
00:42:47.660 --> 00:42:53.164
Contention: version conflicts or retries per slot, p99 of hold commands.

609
00:42:53.444 --> 00:42:57.945
Read model drift: sampled comparison with event replay; projector lag.

610
00:42:58.225 --> 00:43:05.735
Business: fill rate by hour, lost customers (slot full, no alternative chosen), in-slot delivery rate, fee revenue.

611
00:43:07.085 --> 00:43:08.642
Security and privacy.

612
00:43:08.922 --> 00:43:15.599
Slot events contain customer ids and addresses by reference only; keep PII in the order service.

613
00:43:15.879 --> 00:43:22.727
Ops actions (close slot, change capacity) are events with actor ids, which gives an audit trail for free.

614
00:43:23.007 --> 00:43:29.023
Rate-limit hold attempts per customer to stop bots hoarding festival slots (Ch 39).

615
00:43:30.373 --> 00:43:31.269
Performance.

616
00:43:31.549 --> 00:43:35.880
Listing from Redis per zone (16 slots times 7 days in one hash).

617
00:43:36.160 --> 00:43:42.624
Hold path: snapshot + tail load, one insert; p99 dominated by hot-slot conflicts.

618
00:43:42.904 --> 00:43:47.256
Projection rebuild is parallelisable by slot (partition by zone).

619
00:43:48.606 --> 00:43:49.331
Cost.

620
00:43:49.611 --> 00:43:56.225
The event store is small (~200 MB per day); keep 90 days hot and archive the rest to the lake.

621
00:43:56.505 --> 00:44:04.611
The real cost is rider time: accurate capacity (L1) and demand shaping (L4) outweigh any infrastructure saving.

622
00:44:04.891 --> 00:44:10.331
Peak fees can fund shoulder discounts (L4: net positive under the stated assumptions).

623
00:44:11.681 --> 00:44:12.620
Part 7.

624
00:44:12.900 --> 00:44:14.607
Anti-Patterns and Pitfalls.

625
00:44:15.857 --> 00:44:16.774
Watch out:

626
00:44:17.054 --> 00:44:17.694
1.

627
00:44:17.974 --> 00:44:19.766
Capacity as an order count.

628
00:44:20.046 --> 00:44:22.606
Orders differ threefold in cost (L1).

629
00:44:23.956 --> 00:44:24.873
Watch out:

630
00:44:25.153 --> 00:44:25.857
2.

631
00:44:26.137 --> 00:44:27.908
One capacity for every hour.

632
00:44:28.188 --> 00:44:30.364
The roster and traffic change by hour.

633
00:44:31.714 --> 00:44:32.631
Watch out:

634
00:44:32.911 --> 00:44:33.658
3.

635
00:44:33.938 --> 00:44:35.901
Read, check in code, write back.

636
00:44:36.181 --> 00:44:39.722
Overbooks under concurrency (L2: all 60 slots).

637
00:44:41.072 --> 00:44:41.989
Watch out:

638
00:44:42.269 --> 00:44:42.973
4.

639
00:44:43.253 --> 00:44:45.408
SERIALIZABLE as a blanket fix.

640
00:44:45.688 --> 00:44:47.608
Correct but aborts on hot rows.

641
00:44:47.888 --> 00:44:49.595
Prefer an atomic statement.

642
00:44:50.945 --> 00:44:51.862
Watch out:

643
00:44:52.142 --> 00:44:52.910
5.

644
00:44:53.190 --> 00:44:54.726
Holds without expiry.

645
00:44:55.006 --> 00:44:57.395
Abandoned checkouts keep slots full forever.

646
00:44:58.745 --> 00:44:59.663
Watch out:

647
00:44:59.943 --> 00:45:00.689
6.

648
00:45:00.969 --> 00:45:03.401
Releasing the old slot before booking the new.

649
00:45:03.681 --> 00:45:05.601
A failed reschedule loses both.

650
00:45:06.951 --> 00:45:07.869
Watch out:

651
00:45:08.149 --> 00:45:08.895
7.

652
00:45:09.175 --> 00:45:11.565
A mutable counter as the only record.

653
00:45:11.845 --> 00:45:14.490
It can't be audited or recomputed (L3).

654
00:45:15.840 --> 00:45:16.757
Watch out:

655
00:45:17.037 --> 00:45:17.720
8.

656
00:45:18.000 --> 00:45:20.389
Loading busy aggregates by full replay.

657
00:45:20.669 --> 00:45:23.293
Thousands of rows per booking; use snapshots.

658
00:45:24.643 --> 00:45:25.561
Watch out:

659
00:45:25.841 --> 00:45:26.587
9.

660
00:45:26.867 --> 00:45:29.171
Snapshots written in the booking transaction.

661
00:45:29.451 --> 00:45:31.393
It adds latency to the hot path.

662
00:45:31.673 --> 00:45:33.166
Write them asynchronously.

663
00:45:34.516 --> 00:45:35.433
Watch out:

664
00:45:35.713 --> 00:45:36.417
10.

665
00:45:36.697 --> 00:45:38.148
Greying out full slots.

666
00:45:38.428 --> 00:45:41.095
The customer does the search and leaves (L4).

667
00:45:42.445 --> 00:45:43.362
Watch out:

668
00:45:43.642 --> 00:45:44.474
11.

669
00:45:44.754 --> 00:45:47.463
Availability that ignores this order's size.

670
00:45:47.743 --> 00:45:49.855
It shows slots that will reject the hold.

671
00:45:51.205 --> 00:45:52.123
Watch out:

672
00:45:52.403 --> 00:45:53.192
12.

673
00:45:53.472 --> 00:45:55.563
Rosters planned without booking data.

674
00:45:55.843 --> 00:46:00.024
Riders sit idle in the afternoon while evening customers are turned away.

675
00:46:01.374 --> 00:46:02.249
Part 8.

676
00:46:02.529 --> 00:46:03.873
Interview Q and A.

677
00:46:05.123 --> 00:46:05.955
Beginner.

678
00:46:06.235 --> 00:46:07.259
Question 1.

679
00:46:07.539 --> 00:46:10.952
Design a delivery slot booking system, the main components?

680
00:46:11.232 --> 00:46:12.597
Thirty-second answer:

681
00:46:12.877 --> 00:46:32.589
A slot service that owns slot templates and capacity; a booking path that holds capacity atomically during checkout and confirms on payment; expiry of abandoned holds; a read model and cache for fast listings; events to dispatch and stores; capacity fed by rosters and cost estimates per order.

682
00:46:33.939 --> 00:46:34.942
Question 2.

683
00:46:35.222 --> 00:46:36.865
How do you prevent overbooking?

684
00:46:37.145 --> 00:46:38.510
Thirty-second answer:

685
00:46:38.790 --> 00:46:40.454
Make check-and-take atomic:

686
00:46:40.734 --> 00:46:45.065
UPDATE slot SET used = used + m WHERE id =?

687
00:46:45.345 --> 00:46:55.905
AND used + m = cap (row lock serialises, a failed condition means full), or optimistic versioned appends where a unique key rejects concurrent writers.

688
00:46:56.185 --> 00:46:58.595
Never read, check in code, and write back.

689
00:46:59.945 --> 00:47:00.991
Question 3.

690
00:47:01.271 --> 00:47:03.468
What's the right unit of slot capacity?

691
00:47:03.748 --> 00:47:05.113
Thirty-second answer:

692
00:47:05.393 --> 00:47:16.465
Whatever actually runs out: rider-minutes from the roster (and picker-minutes in the store), with each order deducting its estimated cost based on distance, building, basket and hour.

693
00:47:16.745 --> 00:47:20.116
Order counts ignore that orders differ threefold in cost.

694
00:47:21.466 --> 00:47:22.533
Question 4.

695
00:47:22.813 --> 00:47:24.690
How do slot listings stay fast?

696
00:47:24.970 --> 00:47:26.335
Thirty-second answer:

697
00:47:26.615 --> 00:47:37.346
Serve them from a read model cached in Redis per zone (free minutes per slot), updated by a projector, accepting seconds of staleness; the booking command does the exact check.

698
00:47:37.626 --> 00:47:40.037
Filter by this order's estimated cost.

699
00:47:41.387 --> 00:47:42.389
Intermediate.

700
00:47:42.669 --> 00:47:43.779
Question 5.

701
00:47:44.059 --> 00:47:46.661
How do holds work, and how long should they last?

702
00:47:46.941 --> 00:47:48.307
Thirty-second answer:

703
00:47:48.587 --> 00:47:55.264
A hold reserves the order's minutes at checkout; payment confirms it; a TTL expires abandoned holds.

704
00:47:55.544 --> 00:48:04.675
Keep the TTL short (a few minutes) and extend it when payment actually starts, so abandoned carts don't block slots but slow payers aren't punished.

705
00:48:06.025 --> 00:48:07.134
Question 6.

706
00:48:07.414 --> 00:48:09.099
How do you handle rescheduling?

707
00:48:09.379 --> 00:48:10.745
Thirty-second answer:

708
00:48:11.025 --> 00:48:17.702
Book the new slot first (hold + confirm), then release the old one; if the new booking fails, the old stays.

709
00:48:17.982 --> 00:48:21.609
Across aggregates, it's a small saga with idempotent steps.

710
00:48:22.959 --> 00:48:24.047
Question 7.

711
00:48:24.327 --> 00:48:27.441
What happens when capacity drops after slots are booked?

712
00:48:27.721 --> 00:48:29.087
Thirty-second answer:

713
00:48:29.367 --> 00:48:44.940
A CAPACITYCHANGED event can make a slot over-committed: stop new holds, keep existing bookings, alert ops, let dispatch borrow riders from neighbouring zones, and proactively offer affected customers a move with an incentive if the gap remains.

714
00:48:46.290 --> 00:48:47.314
Question 8.

715
00:48:47.594 --> 00:48:49.386
Why might you event-source a slot?

716
00:48:49.666 --> 00:48:51.031
Thirty-second answer:

717
00:48:51.311 --> 00:49:04.282
The slot's history (holds, bookings, expiries, cancellations, capacity changes) is the business record: it answers audit questions, lets you rebuild read models after bugs, and supports time travel.

718
00:49:04.562 --> 00:49:08.189
Version-guarded appends also give optimistic concurrency.

719
00:49:09.539 --> 00:49:10.605
Question 9.

720
00:49:10.885 --> 00:49:12.955
Why do busy slots need snapshots?

721
00:49:13.235 --> 00:49:14.600
Thirty-second answer:

722
00:49:14.880 --> 00:49:22.133
Every command loads the aggregate by replaying its events; hot slots accumulate thousands of holds and expiries.

723
00:49:22.413 --> 00:49:25.720
A snapshot every N events bounds the replay to the tail.

724
00:49:27.070 --> 00:49:27.838
Senior.

725
00:49:28.118 --> 00:49:29.163
Question 10.

726
00:49:29.443 --> 00:49:34.265
Conditional update, SERIALIZABLE, or optimistic versioning, which would you choose?

727
00:49:34.545 --> 00:49:35.910
Thirty-second answer:

728
00:49:36.190 --> 00:49:43.251
A conditional update for a single-row capacity check (simplest, no retries, best p99 in L2).

729
00:49:43.531 --> 00:49:49.633
Optimistic versioning when the aggregate is event-sourced or the invariant spans more than one column.

730
00:49:49.913 --> 00:49:55.801
SERIALIZABLE when the invariant spans multiple rows and rewriting is hard, accepting aborts.

731
00:49:57.151 --> 00:49:58.281
Question 11.

732
00:49:58.561 --> 00:50:00.951
How would you handle festival slot openings?

733
00:50:01.231 --> 00:50:02.596
Thirty-second answer:

734
00:50:02.876 --> 00:50:18.812
Pre-generate slots, warm caches, use a waiting room if needed (Ch 46 L4), rate-limit per customer, put a Redis admission gate in front of the hot slots, keep bookings atomic in the database, and show alternatives immediately on failure.

735
00:50:20.162 --> 00:50:21.250
Question 12.

736
00:50:21.530 --> 00:50:24.282
How do you estimate an order's cost in capacity?

737
00:50:24.562 --> 00:50:25.927
Thirty-second answer:

738
00:50:26.207 --> 00:50:35.381
Use the ETA models: ride time for distance and hour, door time per building, extra time for bulky baskets, picking time from basket size.

739
00:50:35.661 --> 00:50:41.911
Compute the cost per candidate slot (because traffic differs), and calibrate against actual rider-minutes.

740
00:50:43.261 --> 00:50:44.541
Question 13.

741
00:50:44.821 --> 00:50:47.040
How do you move customers away from the peak?

742
00:50:47.320 --> 00:50:48.685
Thirty-second answer:

743
00:50:48.965 --> 00:51:00.464
Recommend the nearest free slots (earlier too) instead of greying out full ones; price the peak and discount shoulders; offer green (batched) slot discounts; and shift rosters toward demand.

744
00:51:00.744 --> 00:51:03.155
Validate acceptance rates with experiments.

745
00:51:04.505 --> 00:51:05.699
Question 14.

746
00:51:05.979 --> 00:51:07.942
How do you keep the read model correct?

747
00:51:08.222 --> 00:51:09.587
Thirty-second answer:

748
00:51:09.867 --> 00:51:23.521
Idempotent projectors keyed by (slot, version); monitor lag; sample-compare the read model against event replay daily; version projectors and rebuild from events on bugs; blue-green projections for big changes.

749
00:51:24.871 --> 00:51:25.767
Architect.

750
00:51:26.047 --> 00:51:27.241
Question 15.

751
00:51:27.521 --> 00:51:31.703
Design slot-service for 1,500 zones end to end.

752
00:51:31.983 --> 00:51:33.348
Thirty-second answer:

753
00:51:33.628 --> 00:51:54.257
Capacity in minutes from rosters and picking; event-sourced slots with version-guarded appends and async snapshots; projections to a Redis listing cache; holds with lease TTLs; outbox to Kafka for dispatch, stores and notifications; ETA-based costs; recommendations and fees for demand; audits and rebuild tooling.

754
00:51:55.607 --> 00:51:56.802
Question 16.

755
00:51:57.082 --> 00:52:00.709
How does slot-service interact with checkout, dispatch and stores?

756
00:52:00.989 --> 00:52:02.354
Thirty-second answer:

757
00:52:02.634 --> 00:52:15.967
Checkout lists, holds and confirms; payment outcomes drive confirm or expire; booked events feed dispatch planning and store picking waves; roster changes feed capacity; the ETA service feeds costs.

758
00:52:16.247 --> 00:52:18.530
Contracts are events with versioned schemas.

759
00:52:19.880 --> 00:52:21.224
Question 17.

760
00:52:21.504 --> 00:52:23.765
Generalise this to other booking systems.

761
00:52:24.045 --> 00:52:25.411
Thirty-second answer:

762
00:52:25.691 --> 00:52:46.939
Tickets, hotels, appointments and tables all follow the same pattern: define the unit of capacity (seats, room-nights, clinician minutes); take it atomically; hold with TTL; confirm on payment; keep history; serve availability from a cache; handle overbooking policy explicitly (airlines overbook on purpose, using no-show models).

763
00:52:48.289 --> 00:52:49.462
Question 18.

764
00:52:49.742 --> 00:52:51.662
Would you ever overbook deliberately?

765
00:52:51.942 --> 00:52:53.307
Thirty-second answer:

766
00:52:53.587 --> 00:53:08.094
Possibly, if data shows reliable cancellations or no-shows: set the utilisation target above 100% of expected consumption, with a model of cancellations and a recovery plan (borrow riders, incentives).

767
00:53:08.374 --> 00:53:14.390
For groceries, lateness is costly, so most teams keep a buffer below 100% instead.

768
00:53:15.740 --> 00:53:16.977
Question 19.

769
00:53:17.257 --> 00:53:20.969
How would you evolve from a counter to event sourcing without downtime?

770
00:53:21.249 --> 00:53:22.615
Thirty-second answer:

771
00:53:22.895 --> 00:53:38.916
Start writing events alongside the counter (dual write in one transaction), backfill synthetic opening events per slot, build projections and compare with counters, switch reads, then switch commands to version-guarded appends, and keep the counter as a projection.

772
00:53:40.266 --> 00:53:41.375
Question 20.

773
00:53:41.655 --> 00:53:43.725
Which metrics tell you slots are healthy?

774
00:53:44.005 --> 00:53:45.370
Thirty-second answer:

775
00:53:45.650 --> 00:54:01.693
In-slot delivery rate; overbooking audit violations; lost customers (full, no alternative); fill rate by hour versus roster; hold confirm and expiry rates; conflicts and p99 of hold commands; projector lag and drift.

776
00:54:03.043 --> 00:54:03.939
Part 9.

777
00:54:04.219 --> 00:54:05.627
Summary and Practice.

778
00:54:06.877 --> 00:54:07.965
Key takeaways.

779
00:54:08.245 --> 00:54:13.791
Capacity is measured in minutes of what runs out; each order deducts its own cost (L1).

780
00:54:14.071 --> 00:54:19.362
Take capacity atomically: a conditional update or a version-guarded append (L2).

781
00:54:19.642 --> 00:54:26.127
Event-sourced slots give audit, rebuilds and time travel; snapshots keep busy slots fast (L3).

782
00:54:26.407 --> 00:54:31.399
Shape demand with recommendations and prices, and roster to demand (L4).

783
00:54:31.679 --> 00:54:34.410
Listings can be stale; bookings must be exact.

784
00:54:35.760 --> 00:54:37.083
Decision cheat sheet.

785
00:54:37.363 --> 00:54:38.707
Here is a quick table:

786
00:54:38.987 --> 00:54:40.160
Need, and Choice.

787
00:54:40.440 --> 00:54:41.571
Capacity unit:

788
00:54:41.851 --> 00:54:44.027
Rider or picker-minutes per zone-slot.

789
00:54:44.307 --> 00:54:45.053
Booking:

790
00:54:45.333 --> 00:54:49.195
Conditional UPDATE, or event append with PK(slot, version).

791
00:54:49.475 --> 00:54:50.264
Holds:

792
00:54:50.544 --> 00:54:53.232
Short TTL, extended when payment starts.

793
00:54:53.512 --> 00:54:54.344
History:

794
00:54:54.624 --> 00:54:57.461
Event store (or counter + append-only log).

795
00:54:57.741 --> 00:54:58.915
Busy aggregates:

796
00:54:59.195 --> 00:55:02.843
Snapshots every ~100 events, written asynchronously.

797
00:55:03.123 --> 00:55:04.040
Listings:

798
00:55:04.320 --> 00:55:07.691
Redis per zone, seconds-stale, filtered by order cost.

799
00:55:07.971 --> 00:55:08.781
Demand:

800
00:55:09.061 --> 00:55:12.923
Nearest alternatives, peak fees, shoulder discounts, rostering.

801
